    "body": "Note that sometimes stacking contexts are used as a form of content security.\r\n\r\nFor example, in wikipedia, the body of an article can use any HTML/css, but because it explicitly has a `z-index` of `0`, it creates a stacking context, and if with pure CSS the internal content could override that context and appear in the top layer, a user-generated piece of content could occlude important navigation and banners. I am sure more places in the web that include user-generated content would incur that problem if stacking contexts were overridable from the inside.\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/729614037/reactions",
